  "answer": "For a moving logo, the supplied passages support this checklist:\n\n**Manual practice — not legislation**\n1. State in the application that the sign claimed is a **moving image**. [TMM/Part10/3/3/6/1]\n2. Provide a **graphical representation**. It should clearly demonstrate the nature of the mark and show each feature clearly enough for examination. [TMM/Part10/3/3/1; TMM/Part21/2#1]\n3. Accompany the non-traditional sign with a **concise and accurate description**. Under the Manual, the description is entered as an endorsement and helps define the scope of registration together with the graphical representation. [TMM/Part10/3/3/1]\n\n**Legislation**\n- Section 40 of the Act requires rejection if the trade mark cannot be represented graphically. [TMA1995/s40]\n- Regulation 4.3(7) expressly requires a concise and accurate description where the mark contains or consists of colour, scent, shape, sound, an aspect of packaging, or a combination of those features. That provision does not expressly list moving images; the broader requirement for other non-traditional signs appears in the Manual practice quoted above. [TMR1995/r4.3(7); TMM/Part10/3/3/1]\n\n**Limit of these passages:** they do not specify how to depict the motion itself—for example, whether sequential frames, arrows, timing information or a video file are required. I cannot establish those motion-specific requirements from the supplied material."
